    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125989,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 139,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:23:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125990,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 139,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>Patients with melanocytic matricoma present with a well-circumscribed dermal nodule composed of solid aggregates of basophilic matrical and supramatrical cells with scant cytoplasm, round nuclei, prominent nucleoli, and frequent mitotic figures {30133753}. Small clusters of shadow cells may be seen at the centre of the aggregates. Neoplastic cell aggregates contain numerous scattered pigmented dendritic melanocytes and deposits of melanin. With rare exceptions {17598856}, there is no connection with the epidermis or hair follicles, although consumption of the epidermis overlying the tumour has been described {29095740}. Rare tumours contain atypical melanocytes {25678301} or epithelial cells {26730694}.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>The matrical cells express PHLDA1 and nuclear &beta;-catenin, but not BerEP4 {24673383; 17518772; 24641267}. Intratumoural melanocytes express S100, HMB45 antigen (PMEL [gp100]), melan-A, and SOX10 {25678301; 24641267}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>The differential diagnosis includes pigmented pilomatricoma&nbsp;/ pilomatrical carcinoma and basal cell carcinoma with matrical differentiation {35195926}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:24:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
